A 74-year-old man with a history of multiple squamous cell carcinomas that were treated with Mohs micrographic surgery presented with a tender, enlarging scalp lesion of 6 months' duration.

Skin examination revealed a 5-mm bright pink, dome-shaped papule with overlying vessels located at the crown of the scalp. There was no purulent discharge or overlying skin changes.

{'Histopathology': {'Biopsy Findings': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining demonstrated concentric proliferation of ovoid tumor cells present around small vessels over a hyalinized background stroma. The cells were hyperchromatic and variable in nuclear size, with scattered mitoses and necrosis.', 'Immunohistochemistry': '- Smooth muscle actin (SMA) was diffusely positive in the tumor cells\n- CD31 immunostain highlighted many endothelial-lined vascular channels\n- Negative staining for: smooth muscle myosin, h-caldesmon, calponin, desmin, Kaposi sarcoma–associated herpesvirus, SOX10, human melanoma black, S-100, and STAT6', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Bright pink, dome-shaped papule of the scalp crown with overlying vessels measuring 5 mm', 'Figure B': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining showing dermal-based tumor cells arranged in a vague nodular configuration', 'Figure C': 'Proliferation of tumor cells present around small vessels with scattered mitoses and necrosis', 'Figure D': 'Smooth muscle actin stain'}}}

A 7-year-old boy presented with a 2-year history of progressive eruption of papules and nodules all over his body. The lesions did not cause pain or itching. The patient had no family history of cutaneous disorders.

Multiple yellow to reddish brown firm papules and nodules were present on the scalp, face, neck, trunk, and limbs, including the axillae and inguinal regions. The nasal and oropharyngeal mucosa were also affected. The lesions were 2 to 10 mm in diameter with coalescing, yellow-brown nodules and plaques up to 4 cm in diameter on the face, trunk, and limbs.

{'Laboratory Tests': 'Routine laboratory test results and serum chemistry with serum lipid levels were within normal ranges.', 'Imaging Studies': 'No clinical evidence of internal involvement was found on ultrasonography, computed tomography, or magnetic resonance imaging results.', 'Histopathological Examination': {'Findings': 'Dense diffuse cellular infiltrate composed mainly of foamy histiocytic cells and multinucleated Touton cells mixed with lymphocytes and eosinophils. Spindle cells and increased numbers of collagen fibers in a storiform arrangement were visible in the deep dermis.', 'Immunohistochemical Staining': 'Positive for CD68 and CD163, but negative for S100, CD1a, and Langerin.', 'Images': {'Figure 1': 'Clinical images showing disseminated, yellowish, xanthomatous maculopapular lesions and nodules before and after treatment', 'Figure 2': 'Histopathologic and immunohistochemical examination findings showing cellular infiltration and staining results'}}}

A 55-year-old man with severe dilated cardiomyopathy and a left ventricular assist device (LVAD; HeartMate II) implanted four years previously was admitted for evaluation of low-grade fever. His serum creatinine had increased from 124 μmol/L to 354 μmol/L over two months, and he developed urinary protein excretion of 2.2 g/day with microhaematuria.

Physical examination was unremarkable.

