A 52-year-old man with HCV-associated end-stage cirrhosis underwent liver transplantation. Post-surgery, he required relaparotomy the next day due to intra-abdominal bleeding from a choledochal artery branch. Initial liver function improved, but on day 4, cholestasis was observed with worsening cytolysis. On day 6, the patient reported persistent abdominal pain. By day 7, he developed fulminant liver failure and hemodynamic shock. On day 8, he experienced a single fever peak (38.4°C). The patient died on day 9 during laparotomy.

Physical examination findings were not explicitly detailed in the case report.

